    - target: {fileID: 3225774331078614319, guid: 2f4d987e3e947ad4ea7d3109f029e81e, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Text
      value: Mangrove Forests make up one of the most plentiful plant populations
        in the Everglade Ecosystem. They are present near the coastal channels and
        winding rivers around the tip of South Florida. The term "mangrove" does
        not refer to a particular botanical relation, but rather is used to identify
        several species of salt-tolerant trees that thrive amidst the harsh growing
        conditions of the coast. These come in three varieties; Red mangroves (Rhizophora
        mangle), known for their stilt-like roots, Black mangroves (Aveciennia germinans)
        and white mangroves (Laguncularia racemosa) which thrive within tidal waters,
        where freshwater from the Everglades mixes with saltwater. The Everglades
        boasts the largest contigious stand of protected mangrove forest in the Western
        Hemisphere.
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 3225774331078614319, guid: 2f4d987e3e947ad4ea7d3109f029e81e, type: 3}
